WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.475
How can the same set of pins have three different wirelengths?

00:00:03.475 --> 00:00:09.225
In this lesson, we use three different rulers to understand net models in chip
placement.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.762
A net on a chip can connect several pins at once.

00:00:14.762 --> 00:00:18.875
The connections are already specified; placement chooses their positions.

00:00:18.875 --> 00:00:22.950
Before estimating wirelength, we must also choose a measurement model.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:31.638
Start with four pins at the corners of a square of side two.

00:00:31.638 --> 00:00:36.950
We use these same coordinates throughout, comparing a bounding box, a clique, and a
star.

00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:47.208
We use Manhattan distance, rather than straight-line distance. Add the absolute
horizontal difference to the absolute vertical difference.

00:00:47.208 --> 00:00:50.021
The bent lines in our drawings illustrate that measurement.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:58.250
From A at the lower left to D at the upper right, move two units horizontally and two
vertically. Their Manhattan distance is four.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:03.162
Every model here uses this same basic distance.

00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:08.596
The first model finds the smallest box containing all pins.

00:01:08.596 --> 00:01:13.058
The leftmost and rightmost pins determine its width. The lowest and highest determine
its height.

00:01:13.058 --> 00:01:16.033
Add width and height.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:24.613
For our square, width and height are both two, giving a half-perimeter wirelength of
four.

00:01:24.613 --> 00:01:29.750
Walking around all four sides gives eight. That is the full perimeter, not this
metric.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.125
The bounding box needs only four extreme coordinates.

00:01:35.125 --> 00:01:38.663
Scan each pin once to find them, so the computation grows linearly with the number of
pins.

00:01:38.663 --> 00:01:43.363
It is a cheap wirelength estimate.

00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:51.771
The second model is a clique: connect every pair of distinct pins and add their
Manhattan distances.

00:01:51.771 --> 00:01:55.621
Four pins give six unordered pairs. Count each pair exactly once.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:05.813
In alphabetical order, measure A to B, A to C, and A to D; then B to C, B to D, and C
to D.

00:02:05.813 --> 00:02:10.688
The six distances are two, two, four, four, two, and two.

00:02:11.967 --> 00:02:15.992
Adding these six terms gives sixteen.

00:02:15.992 --> 00:02:22.942
Do not count both A to B and B to A. Those names describe the same unordered pair;
double counting would incorrectly produce thirty-two.

00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:29.946
With p pins, a clique has p times p minus one, divided by two edges.

00:02:29.946 --> 00:02:36.858
Here we sum unnormalized Manhattan distances. This is not the squared-distance
objective used in quadratic placement.

00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:40.371
The third model is a star.

00:02:40.371 --> 00:02:44.971
Choose one existing pin as a hub and connect it to every other pin.

00:02:44.971 --> 00:02:49.271
Our example uses A. We do not introduce a virtual center.

00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:56.642
With A as the hub, the spokes are A to B, A to C, and A to D.

00:02:56.642 --> 00:03:00.642
Their lengths are two, two, and four, totaling eight.

00:03:00.642 --> 00:03:04.029
Other pin pairs do not enter this sum.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:10.813
A star has only p minus one spokes, so its computation is linear in the pin count.

00:03:10.813 --> 00:03:15.800
But the hub is part of the model definition.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.313
Always name it when reporting the result.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.700
The same pins now give four, sixteen, and eight.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.725
Neither their coordinates nor their connections changed. Only the measurement
changed.

00:03:28.725 --> 00:03:32.350
These numbers alone cannot rank which model is better.

00:03:33.633 --> 00:03:38.658
For the square, choosing any corner as the star hub happens to give the same answer.

00:03:38.658 --> 00:03:41.046
That is a consequence of symmetry.

00:03:41.046 --> 00:03:45.508
An asymmetric example reveals why the hub matters.

00:03:46.767 --> 00:03:55.379
Using A, B, C, and D as hubs now gives sixteen, twenty-two, fourteen, and twenty.

00:03:55.379 --> 00:03:57.117
C has the smallest value here.

00:03:57.117 --> 00:04:02.142
This shows hub dependence; it does not prove a placement algorithm is optimal.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:13.175
Next, A, B, and C support an eight by eight bounding box. Move the interior pin D
from four, four to two, two.

00:04:13.175 --> 00:04:16.450
Watch the three rulers: which readings change?

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:23.062
The half-perimeter stays sixteen because the outer boundaries did not change.

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:28.375
The clique decreases from fifty-six to fifty-two, and the star from twenty-four to
twenty.

00:04:28.375 --> 00:04:31.225
Different models respond differently to the same move.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:37.575
Place every pin at one position and all three wirelengths become zero.

00:04:37.575 --> 00:04:41.162
Real cells have area and cannot freely overlap.

00:04:41.162 --> 00:04:45.638
These measurements have not checked density, overlap, or timing.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:52.675
The bent lines illustrate measurement relationships, not final shared routing paths.

00:04:52.675 --> 00:05:00.800
Before comparing two placement candidates, align the model, the distance, the
weights, and the constraints. Small numbers need a clear definition.

00:05:02.067 --> 00:05:07.067
On the website, load the spread square and then the compact reference square.

00:05:07.067 --> 00:05:13.279
The readings change from sixteen, sixty-four, thirty-two to four, sixteen, eight.

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:16.842
Drag a pin to investigate your own placement.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:23.388
Change the star hub, move the interior pin, and try the six experiment tasks.

00:05:23.388 --> 00:05:30.463
Write down your prediction first. Then inspect each computed contribution and explain
where the change came from.

00:05:31.733 --> 00:05:38.296
Finally, implement the three functions in the downloadable Python exercise and
compare with the independent reference.

00:05:38.296 --> 00:05:45.196
More important than remembering four, sixteen, and eight is explaining exactly what
each number measures.
